1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:11,666
I am the docent. A reviewer or an examiner asks me about a paper or a thesis, and I answer with the exact place in the work: the chapter, the figure, the script.

2
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:21,834
Every answer I give names the place in the work it rests on, and quotes the sentence. When a claim has code, I re-run it before reporting the number.

3
00:00:21,834 --> 00:00:34,890
When the work does not address the question, I say Absent, and name the nearest thing the work does cover. Every question and answer is kept in a log, part of the record of the examination.

4
00:00:35,299 --> 00:00:48,056
I work for both sides. For a reviewer or an examiner, I answer questions about the work. For the author, I help build the package: the work with each claim linked to its code and data.

5
00:00:48,056 --> 00:01:03,527
I point at the work and leave the judging to others: the examiner decides. For the institution's own steps and deadlines, I ask the clerk. I work offline, because a thesis under examination is not public.

6
00:01:03,949 --> 00:01:12,480
A real exchange, on a made-up thesis. The examiner asks me where it shows that serum enlarges colonies, and whether that result holds.

7
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:22,721
I name the chapter and the figure, re-run the script, and get the same numbers. I also say what the text leaves out, and who should judge the test.

8
00:01:23,099 --> 00:01:33,062
Asked about incubation temperature, which the thesis never mentions, I answer Absent, list where I looked, and name the nearest thing the thesis does cover.

9
00:01:33,449 --> 00:01:43,187
To use me, open the package in Claude Code and type your question to the docent. Give me the question in plain words; I work out whether you are the examiner or the author.

10
00:01:43,187 --> 00:02:00,487
The first line is the verdict: Located, Absent, or Incomplete when the package itself is missing something. The detail gives the places and the re-run. Each package carries its own note on where things are, and I read it first.
